    51	
    52	### 3.2 Save and status
    53	
    54	- Save only through **Save** or Ctrl/Cmd+S while editing. Prevent the browser's Save Page action for this shortcut in editing mode.
    55	- No writes on keystrokes, tab switches, navigation, or timers.
    56	- Keep the editor usable while a save is pending.
    57	- Each save captures a draft snapshot and the currently acknowledged hash.
    58	- At most one save for the active document is in flight. A second explicit save during an in-flight request is queued, not sent concurrently.
    59	- Maintain one pending slot: further explicit save actions replace it with their latest requested draft snapshot. Keystrokes alone never enqueue a save.
    60	- After success, send any queued snapshot with the hash returned by that success. Do not send it with the previous hash.
    61	- If a save fails or conflicts, stop the queue. Preserve the current draft; do not automatically retry queued writes.
    62	- Async responses belong to their original document/session. A late response must not alter another file's editor, hash, or status.
    63	
    64	Display these accessible statuses:
    65	
    66	| Status | Meaning |
    67	|---|---|
    68	| Unsaved | Current draft differs from the last acknowledged content and no save is pending |
    69	| Saving | A save request is in flight; do not imply subsequent unsent edits are saved |
    70	| Saved | The acknowledged content equals the current draft and no save is pending |
    71	| Error | The last save failed or conflicted; include actionable recovery guidance |
    72	
    73	After an acknowledgment, use **Unsaved**, not **Saved**, if the user has since changed the draft. A successful HTTP response alone is insufficient to show Saved.
    74	
    75	### 3.3 Failed saves
    76	
    77	- Preserve the complete current draft and any edits made during the failed request.
    78	- Show the failure and offer an explicit retry for ordinary network/server errors, plus Copy draft.
    79	- Retry uses the last acknowledged hash. If the previous write succeeded but its response was lost, retry may correctly return a conflict; never bypass the hash check to hide that uncertainty.
    80	- Never claim a failed or unacknowledged write is saved.
    81	
    82	### 3.4 Conflict recovery
    83	
    84	- A 409 from a content write enters a conflict state, shows a clear explanation, and blocks Save and Revert until Reload succeeds or the edit session is discarded.
    85	- Offer **Reload** and **Copy draft**. Do not automatically merge, overwrite, or adopt the disk hash while keeping an unacknowledged draft writable.
    86	- Reload explicitly warns that the draft will be discarded. Only replace the draft after a successful fresh read; a failed Reload keeps the draft and conflict state.
    87	- Successful Reload establishes a new edit baseline, acknowledged content, and hash.
    88	- Copy draft copies the current draft, not the baseline or last save snapshot. Report success only after the clipboard operation succeeds; provide a selectable-text fallback when unavailable.
    89	
    90	### 3.5 Revert
    91	
    92	- **Revert** means restore the content captured when Edit began **on disk**, not merely undo the last keystroke.
    93	- Confirm that it will discard current draft changes and save the edit-session baseline.
    94	- Submit the baseline through the same save pipeline and latest acknowledged hash check. Revert may itself return 409.
    95	- Disable Revert while a save/queued save is pending or while conflicted. This avoids interleaving a destructive baseline restore with queued writes.
    96	- On success, set draft and acknowledged content to the baseline and retain the returned hash. Ordinary successful saves do not change the original edit-session baseline.
    97	- On failure, retain the pre-Revert draft and show recovery guidance. Reload or leaving/re-entering Edit starts a new baseline.
    98	
    99	## 4. Navigation and recovery limits
   100	
   101	- In-app actions that would abandon a dirty draft prompt **Discard changes?**, with Cancel preserving the draft, URL, focus, and editing state.
   102	- Cover breadcrumbs, Back to folder, opening another file, leaving editing mode, and other app-controlled navigation. Switching Edit/Preview does not discard anything and must not prompt.
   103	- While a write is pending, prevent app-controlled actions that would abandon its session; explain that the save must finish first. Browser history/departure remains outside this guarantee.
   104	- Register `beforeunload` while there are unsaved changes or an unacknowledged save. Remove it once safe. Browsers control whether and how the native warning appears.
   105	- Do not intercept browser Back/Forward. Unsaved drafts, including after a failed save, are lost when history navigation abandons editing; returning must not pretend the discarded draft is retained.
   106	- Abrupt closure can lose unsaved work. A request already sent may complete after departure. Reopening reads the current file from disk.
   107	- Deleted files have no built-in recovery beyond git for fixtures. Document these limits visibly in recovery guidance and the README.
   108	
   109	## 5. File and folder operations
   110	
   111	Provide keyboard-accessible controls from the existing graph/outline browsing context and document view where applicable. Use explicit labelled dialogs; do not add a permanent side pane.
   112	
   113	| Operation | Allowed targets | Behavior |
   114	|---|---|---|
   115	| Create file | Existing folder or source root | Create a `.md` file, empty by default; do not create parent directories implicitly |
   116	| Create folder | Existing folder or source root | Create one empty directory |
   117	| Rename | Markdown file or empty folder | Change the basename within the same parent/source |
   118	| Move | Markdown file or empty folder | Change location within the same source; destination parent must exist |
   119	| Delete | Markdown file or empty folder | Confirm using the full source and relative path; never recurse |
   120	| Copy | Individual Markdown file | Copy to the other source only; preserve file bytes/frontmatter as is |
   121	
   122	Additional requirements:
   123	
   124	- No source-root rename, move, or deletion. An empty relative path is not a mutation target.
   125	- Folder emptiness is checked on disk at execution, including hidden and non-Markdown entries that the browser does not list.
   126	- File destinations must retain a case-insensitive `.md` extension. Names and paths must pass shared validation.
   127	- Destination selectors must not allow arbitrary filesystem roots. The server remains authoritative even if the client validates first.
